 "literal": "And giving and enjoying, to those who do not have, saying they do not exist / the work to be done, to exceed greatly, you think; for that work / alas, the young woman also, will she come? / me, will you take? Please tell, O heart!",
 "translation": "Thinking that giving to others and enjoying life are impossible for those without wealth, you plan to go and perform a task that exceeds all limits. O heart, tell me: will my beautiful young woman also come with us for that task, or will you take only me?",
 "modern": "பிறருக்குக் கொடுப்பதும் நாம் அனுபவிப்பதும் செல்வம் இல்லாதவர்களுக்கு இல்லை என்று எண்ணி, பொருள் ஈட்டும் செயலை எல்லை கடந்து செய்ய நினைக்கிறாய். என் நெஞ்சமே! அந்தச் செயலைச் செய்ய என் அழகிய தலைவியும் வருவாளா? அல்லது என்னை மட்டும் அழைத்துச் செல்வாயா? எனக்குச் சொல்வாயாக.",
 "explanation": "The hero is speaking to his own heart in the context of Pālai tiṇai (separation). He is torn between his duty to earn wealth and his love for his lady. His heart urges him to leave for a distant land to acquire wealth, arguing that without money, one can neither practice charity nor enjoy life's pleasures. However, the hero hesitates, knowing the journey through the harsh desert is too difficult for his delicate beloved. He questions his heart, asking if the young woman will accompany them or if the heart intends to drag him away alone, highlighting his deep inner conflict.",
